1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,801
Two hundred miles a month.

2
00:00:03,891 --> 00:00:06,153
About 320 kilometres.

3
00:00:06,356 --> 00:00:10,810
In the winter of 1956,
that was roughly how far the basic

4
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:14,831
petrol ration let a British
private motorist drive.

5
00:00:15,031 --> 00:00:17,364
In Parliament, an MP asks:

6
00:00:17,602 --> 00:00:20,162
So how much does each person actually get?

7
00:00:20,407 --> 00:00:24,868
The minister answered not in litres,
but in the distance you could drive.

8
00:00:25,067 --> 00:00:28,171
It was a number any
driver could use to count

9
00:00:28,211 --> 00:00:31,455
how many times they could
get to work and back.

10
00:00:31,704 --> 00:00:34,713
But just weeks earlier,
do you know what reason the

11
00:00:34,753 --> 00:00:38,001
British government gave the
public for military action?

12
00:00:38,209 --> 00:00:40,710
To protect the road that brought

13
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:43,331
oil into Europe — the Suez Canal.

14
00:00:43,546 --> 00:00:47,134
British troops really did
reach the mouth of that canal.

15
00:00:47,324 --> 00:00:52,282
Yet the canal was blocked, and the
British were handed ration coupons.

16
00:00:52,479 --> 00:00:55,089
So where did the calculation go wrong?

17
00:00:55,285 --> 00:00:59,872
To find the answer, we first
have to board a French tanker.

18
00:01:00,114 --> 00:01:01,686
Her name was the Brumaire.

19
00:01:01,969 --> 00:01:05,986
She led a line of thirty-one
ships off the coast of Egypt.

20
00:01:06,194 --> 00:01:09,722
Let's start with who was
on her bridge that day —

21
00:01:09,762 --> 00:01:12,926
and why simply standing
there made the news.

22
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:17,372
We go back seven weeks before that voyage.

23
00:01:17,462 --> 00:01:19,961
Alexandria, a city on the Mediterranean.

24
00:01:20,113 --> 00:01:24,216
Egypt's president, Gamal Abdel
Nasser, is giving a speech.

25
00:01:24,306 --> 00:01:27,041
What he had to explain to his people was

26
00:01:27,081 --> 00:01:29,886
a dam on the Nile, in the south of Egypt.

27
00:01:30,053 --> 00:01:32,608
The Aswan High Dam would
store the river's water,

28
00:01:32,648 --> 00:01:35,785
release it when farmers needed it,
and generate electricity.

29
00:01:35,884 --> 00:01:38,478
The plan was big, and it needed money.

30
00:01:38,634 --> 00:01:43,604
But the United States withdrew
its offer to help fund the dam.

31
00:01:43,739 --> 00:01:46,138
Announcing the news, Nasser singled

32
00:01:46,178 --> 00:01:48,646
out a company in the north of Egypt.

33
00:01:48,774 --> 00:01:50,654
It was the Suez Canal Company.

34
00:01:50,848 --> 00:01:54,952
At the time, ships carrying oil from
the Middle East to Europe sailed up the

35
00:01:54,992 --> 00:01:59,206
Red Sea, entered the Suez Canal,
and came straight out into the Mediterranean.

36
00:01:59,296 --> 00:02:01,351
If that route was closed,
they had to sail all

37
00:02:01,391 --> 00:02:03,263
the way around the southern tip of Africa.

38
00:02:03,371 --> 00:02:06,284
The tolls shipping
companies paid to cut that

39
00:02:06,324 --> 00:02:09,172
long voyage short were
the company's income.

40
00:02:09,309 --> 00:02:12,721
On the map, both banks of
the canal are Egyptian land.

41
00:02:12,811 --> 00:02:17,857
But could the Egyptian government spend the
toll revenue as part of its own budget?

42
00:02:17,949 --> 00:02:19,198
It could not.

43
00:02:19,288 --> 00:02:22,156
The company running the waterway answered

44
00:02:22,196 --> 00:02:24,639
to French and British shareholders.

45
00:02:24,789 --> 00:02:29,874
The canal opened in 1869, with an
operating concession of 99 years.

46
00:02:29,964 --> 00:02:33,207
Under the contract,
it would run until 1968.

47
00:02:33,369 --> 00:02:37,296
Nasser declares he will not wait
the remaining twelve years.

48
00:02:37,423 --> 00:02:41,931
He would nationalise the company and build
the dam with the money the canal earned.

49
00:02:42,089 --> 00:02:45,504
The decree even included a clause
compensating shareholders at

50
00:02:45,544 --> 00:02:48,903
the previous day's closing price
on the Paris stock exchange.

51
00:02:49,082 --> 00:02:51,749
While the president was
still speaking, Egyptian

52
00:02:51,789 --> 00:02:54,739
officials began taking over
the company's operations.

53
00:02:54,843 --> 00:02:58,006
That evening, the owner
named on paper changed.

54
00:02:58,144 --> 00:03:00,995
So from the next day on,
every time a large ship

55
00:03:01,035 --> 00:03:03,585
arrived, who would guide
it safely through?

56
00:03:03,704 --> 00:03:06,957
That was exactly what Egypt had to prove.

57
00:03:08,597 --> 00:03:10,715
It is the morning of September 15.

58
00:03:10,805 --> 00:03:15,203
At the southern entrance of the canal,
the white French tanker Brumaire moves in.

59
00:03:15,376 --> 00:03:17,404
Thirty more ships follow behind her.

60
00:03:17,494 --> 00:03:20,884
There were British ships,
Japanese ships, American ships.

61
00:03:21,174 --> 00:03:23,464
Their destination: Port Said,
at the northern end,

62
00:03:23,504 --> 00:03:25,328
where the canal meets the Mediterranean.

63
00:03:25,677 --> 00:03:28,555
The night before, many of
the foreign pilots who had

64
00:03:28,595 --> 00:03:31,473
worked for the old canal
company walked off the job.

65
00:03:31,762 --> 00:03:35,107
A pilot knows the waterway
intimately and helps the

66
00:03:35,147 --> 00:03:38,359
captain take a big ship
through narrow stretches.

67
00:03:38,631 --> 00:03:41,358
Even a captain with decades at sea listens

68
00:03:41,398 --> 00:03:43,862
to the pilot in an unfamiliar channel.

69
00:03:44,176 --> 00:03:47,214
Because from the surface,
you cannot see how much

70
00:03:47,254 --> 00:03:50,104
room there is between
the hull and the bottom.

71
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:52,927
The wire reporter Gerald Aratoon, filing

72
00:03:52,967 --> 00:03:55,621
from Suez at the time,
described the scene.

73
00:03:55,920 --> 00:03:58,707
Mahmoud Younes, who was
running the canal, checked

74
00:03:58,747 --> 00:04:01,761
preparations with other
officials late into the night.

75
00:04:02,028 --> 00:04:06,539
In the morning, he is said to have shaken
hands with each pilot as they boarded.

76
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:09,837
One of them climbs up to
the Brumaire's bridge.

77
00:04:10,154 --> 00:04:15,138
The ship was French, but the man reading
the channel at the very front was Egyptian.

78
00:04:15,465 --> 00:04:20,208
Egyptian pilots and pilots from other
countries took the ships that followed.

79
00:04:20,514 --> 00:04:23,998
In the past, they would switch
at the midpoint, Ismailia,

80
00:04:24,038 --> 00:04:27,398
when possible — that day,
most had to go the whole way.

81
00:04:27,728 --> 00:04:30,970
The whistle sounds,
and the lead ship moves north.

82
00:04:31,249 --> 00:04:34,923
From the other end, thirteen ships
bound south enter separately.

83
00:04:35,212 --> 00:04:40,076
Harder than getting the first
ship through once is taking the

84
00:04:40,116 --> 00:04:44,820
ships that come tomorrow,
and doing it again the day after.

85
00:04:45,093 --> 00:04:48,048
Let's look at the Egyptian
canal authority's records.

86
00:04:48,253 --> 00:04:50,075
On September 16, 36 ships passed through.

87
00:04:50,349 --> 00:04:50,917
The next day, 35.

88
00:04:51,007 --> 00:04:52,106
The day after that, 32.

89
00:04:52,390 --> 00:04:54,789
The numbers are almost boringly similar.

90
00:04:54,982 --> 00:04:56,773
And that was the news.

91
00:04:57,069 --> 00:04:59,989
There had been fears that
under Egypt the ships would

92
00:05:00,029 --> 00:05:03,005
grind to a halt in long queues —
but they kept moving.

93
00:05:03,304 --> 00:05:06,848
So while the ships were moving,
was the diplomatic problem being solved?

94
00:05:07,165 --> 00:05:11,549
A month later, representatives of
three countries met near Paris.

95
00:05:11,829 --> 00:05:14,284
There was no seat for
Egypt at that meeting.

96
00:05:15,924 --> 00:05:17,722
Sèvres, near Paris.

97
00:05:17,916 --> 00:05:20,844
On October 24, representatives of Britain,

98
00:05:20,884 --> 00:05:23,812
France and Israel sign a secret agreement.

99
00:05:23,963 --> 00:05:26,723
Read this document with the
date covered, and it looks

100
00:05:26,763 --> 00:05:29,626
like a report summing up what
happened a few days later.

101
00:05:29,727 --> 00:05:32,255
But it was signed five
days before the war began.

102
00:05:32,345 --> 00:05:33,839
The three countries came to that
room for different reasons.

103
00:05:34,369 --> 00:05:40,554
For Britain, Middle Eastern oil and
long-held influence were at stake.

104
00:05:40,644 --> 00:05:44,930
France was furious that Nasser
was backing the Algerian

105
00:05:44,970 --> 00:05:49,020
independence movement fighting
French colonial rule.

106
00:05:49,110 --> 00:05:51,743
Israel had endured border
clashes with Egypt,

107
00:05:51,783 --> 00:05:54,653
and wanted to secure its
sea lane to the Red Sea.

108
00:05:54,839 --> 00:05:56,622
At Sèvres, those different
aims are tied together.

109
00:05:56,712 --> 00:06:02,367
First, Israel moves into the Sinai
Peninsula — Egyptian territory.

110
00:06:02,457 --> 00:06:05,666
Then Britain and France
would demand that both

111
00:06:05,706 --> 00:06:09,126
sides stop fighting and
pull back from the canal.

112
00:06:09,216 --> 00:06:11,653
A clause was added that
their own troops would

113
00:06:11,693 --> 00:06:14,077
move into key points
along the canal instead.

114
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:18,918
Five days later, on October 29,
Israeli forces invade the Sinai.

115
00:06:19,008 --> 00:06:22,687
The next day, the British and
French ultimatum is issued.

116
00:06:22,777 --> 00:06:25,104
Look at where Egypt's army is on the map.

117
00:06:25,194 --> 00:06:27,313
This is a canal inside its own country.

118
00:06:27,403 --> 00:06:30,337
If Egypt accepted,
it would have to pull its own

119
00:06:30,377 --> 00:06:33,683
troops back and let foreign
forces into its own ports.

120
00:06:33,773 --> 00:06:34,671
And if it refused?

121
00:06:34,761 --> 00:06:37,835
The two countries would have a
pretext to intervene by force.

122
00:06:37,956 --> 00:06:39,826
Egypt refused.

123
00:06:40,141 --> 00:06:43,956
On October 31, British and
French air strikes begin.

124
00:06:44,046 --> 00:06:47,337
On November 5, paratroopers —
and on the 6th, forces landing

125
00:06:47,377 --> 00:06:50,279
from the sea — entered the
northern end of the canal.

126
00:06:50,369 --> 00:06:53,853
In Port Said, buildings
collapsed under bombardment.

127
00:06:53,943 --> 00:06:58,265
The waterway the ships had used was left
full of sunken vessels and obstacles.

128
00:06:58,355 --> 00:07:02,488
In September, the canal stayed open
even after the foreign pilots left.

129
00:07:02,578 --> 00:07:05,278
In November, it was blocked
while the armies of

130
00:07:05,318 --> 00:07:08,017
the countries pledged to
protect it were there.

131
00:07:08,142 --> 00:07:10,400
And that evening,
the British prime minister

132
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:12,698
makes a surprise
announcement in Parliament.

133
00:07:12,788 --> 00:07:16,304
At midnight London time,
the firing would stop.

134
00:07:18,210 --> 00:07:21,070
Why stop on the very
evening of the landing?

135
00:07:21,249 --> 00:07:23,497
Now let's open the books in London.

136
00:07:23,620 --> 00:07:28,324
The man to watch here is Britain's Chancellor
of the Exchequer, Harold Macmillan.

137
00:07:28,589 --> 00:07:32,566
The number he is trying to defend:
one pound to two dollars eighty cents.

138
00:07:32,656 --> 00:07:32,776
It was the official exchange rate
Britain maintained at the time.

139
00:07:32,866 --> 00:07:37,273
Say I sell goods to a British company

140
00:07:37,313 --> 00:07:40,998
and agree to be paid in pounds.

141
00:07:41,088 --> 00:07:43,738
I plan to change the money
into dollars next month,

142
00:07:43,778 --> 00:07:46,428
but it looks like the pound
might fall before then.

143
00:07:46,518 --> 00:07:51,049
So I'd want to lock in dollars a
little earlier, while I still can.

144
00:07:51,380 --> 00:07:54,420
When trades like that pile
up at once, pounds become

145
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:57,382
plentiful, and more people
go looking for dollars.

146
00:07:57,702 --> 00:08:02,129
The Bank of England spent the
dollars it held to buy up pounds.

147
00:08:02,346 --> 00:08:06,134
Thanks to that, the number on the exchange
board could stay close to where it was.

148
00:08:06,396 --> 00:08:09,388
From the outside, it looks
as if nothing has happened.

149
00:08:09,636 --> 00:08:12,879
But to keep the same price
tag up today as yesterday,

150
00:08:12,919 --> 00:08:15,914
it may have to spend more
dollars than yesterday.

151
00:08:16,246 --> 00:08:20,375
Britain's headache was that it
announced its reserves every month.

152
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:23,114
The markets were waiting
for those books too.

153
00:08:23,322 --> 00:08:26,563
The line the authorities wanted
to hold was two billion dollars.

154
00:08:26,778 --> 00:08:29,658
If reserves fell below it, investors would

155
00:08:29,698 --> 00:08:32,231
start counting what Britain had left.

156
00:08:32,518 --> 00:08:34,551
On top of that, the canal was now blocked.

157
00:08:34,641 --> 00:08:37,927
Buying oil was about to
make dollars even scarcer.

158
00:08:38,329 --> 00:08:41,420
Back from Washington in September,
Macmillan expected

159
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:44,610
that American financial help
would be there if needed.

160
00:08:44,866 --> 00:08:47,264
The Americans had promised nothing.

161
00:08:47,612 --> 00:08:50,722
Then, at the end of October,
the invasion of Egypt begins.

162
00:08:50,994 --> 00:08:53,887
The United States called
for a ceasefire at the UN,

163
00:08:53,927 --> 00:08:57,568
and dollars drained from the Bank
of England faster than before.

164
00:08:57,914 --> 00:09:00,666
Had Britain been prepared to let the pound

165
00:09:00,706 --> 00:09:03,125
fall, it would have had more options.

166
00:09:03,381 --> 00:09:06,231
But imported oil could get
more expensive, and the

167
00:09:06,271 --> 00:09:09,295
money many countries held
in pounds would lose value.

168
00:09:09,581 --> 00:09:13,684
The British government and the Bank of
England did not want to pay that price.

169
00:09:13,955 --> 00:09:17,324
In the end, what Macmillan
needed was confidence that,

170
00:09:17,364 --> 00:09:20,480
if dollars ran short,
more could be found outside.

171
00:09:20,673 --> 00:09:23,376
And the very country that
could back that confidence —

172
00:09:23,416 --> 00:09:25,256
the United States — opposed this war.

173
00:09:26,896 --> 00:09:30,369
This episode is often remembered
as the American president ending

174
00:09:30,409 --> 00:09:33,774
the war with a single phone call
to the British prime minister.

175
00:09:34,037 --> 00:09:36,985
President Eisenhower and Prime Minister

176
00:09:37,025 --> 00:09:40,432
Anthony Eden did in fact
speak on November 6.

177
00:09:40,522 --> 00:09:42,048
The record of that call survives.

178
00:09:42,345 --> 00:09:44,985
But the opening is a little strange.

179
00:09:45,125 --> 00:09:47,889
Eisenhower begins by
welcoming the fact that

180
00:09:47,929 --> 00:09:50,502
Eden has been able to accept a ceasefire.

181
00:09:50,765 --> 00:09:54,997
By the time the call was connected,
the ceasefire decision had already been made.

182
00:09:55,192 --> 00:09:59,136
What the two men discussed were
the conditions that followed.

183
00:09:59,485 --> 00:10:03,458
Eden raises the question of engineers
to clear obstacles from the canal.

184
00:10:03,608 --> 00:10:06,404
Eisenhower repeats that those talks

185
00:10:06,444 --> 00:10:08,753
must not delay the ceasefire.

186
00:10:09,048 --> 00:10:11,736
Eden confirms that,
unless attacked, British

187
00:10:11,776 --> 00:10:14,526
forces will stop firing
at midnight that day.

188
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:18,046
So when did the financial
pressure come into play?

189
00:10:18,330 --> 00:10:22,169
In memoirs written later,
Macmillan said he was told during

190
00:10:22,209 --> 00:10:25,786
a cabinet meeting that the
US would not support Britain

191
00:10:25,826 --> 00:10:29,205
drawing on IMF funds until
it agreed to a ceasefire.

192
00:10:29,518 --> 00:10:33,048
The State Department's documentary
editors noted that they could

193
00:10:33,088 --> 00:10:36,674
not find this request or reply in
their own department's records.

194
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:40,439
So exactly who passed on that warning,

195
00:10:40,479 --> 00:10:43,829
and in what words, is still a blank.

196
00:10:44,173 --> 00:10:48,149
Even what happened in public
shows the position London was in.

197
00:10:48,355 --> 00:10:50,811
The UN General Assembly
demanded a ceasefire.

198
00:10:51,029 --> 00:10:53,308
That same week,
the Soviet Union intervened

199
00:10:53,348 --> 00:10:56,113
militarily in Hungary,
and the US was condemning it.

200
00:10:56,395 --> 00:11:01,251
It was hard for that same America to
shield its own ally inside Egypt.

201
00:11:01,473 --> 00:11:04,520
Britain failed to win the
same ally's support —

202
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:07,541
at the UN, or at the
moment it needed dollars.

203
00:11:07,864 --> 00:11:10,429
Open the House of Commons
record, and the time is

204
00:11:10,469 --> 00:11:12,927
written beside Prime
Minister Eden's statement.

205
00:11:13,186 --> 00:11:14,934
6:04 p.m.

206
00:11:15,264 --> 00:11:17,098
Hearing of the midnight ceasefire plan,

207
00:11:17,138 --> 00:11:19,500
the opposition immediately
asks the next question.

208
00:11:19,772 --> 00:11:23,127
Then does the advance of
our forces also stop here?

209
00:11:23,347 --> 00:11:26,617
Does it also mean our troops
are withdrawing from Egypt?

210
00:11:26,953 --> 00:11:28,659
It was an important question.

211
00:11:28,841 --> 00:11:31,768
Between the day the firing
stopped and the day the

212
00:11:31,808 --> 00:11:34,794
last soldier left lay more
than a month and a half.

213
00:11:35,033 --> 00:11:38,694
To see what that time was like,
let's go back to Port Said.

214
00:11:40,623 --> 00:11:44,395
Midnight in London was two
in the morning in Cairo.

215
00:11:44,514 --> 00:11:48,547
In Parliament, setting that
hour was enough — but for the

216
00:11:48,587 --> 00:11:52,478
wounded in Port Said,
what happened next mattered more.

217
00:11:52,671 --> 00:11:55,697
Records of the International
Committee of the Red Cross

218
00:11:55,737 --> 00:11:58,540
trace how one bundle of
medicine reached this city.

219
00:11:58,814 --> 00:12:01,589
On November 7, the Egyptian
Red Crescent makes an

220
00:12:01,629 --> 00:12:04,462
urgent request for medicines
and medical supplies.

221
00:12:04,693 --> 00:12:07,742
On the 11th, a plane carrying four tonnes,

222
00:12:07,782 --> 00:12:11,346
mostly medical supplies,
leaves Geneva for Cairo.

223
00:12:11,584 --> 00:12:14,962
From here to Port Said is a
domestic Egyptian stretch.

224
00:12:15,052 --> 00:12:17,724
But this last leg was the hard part.

225
00:12:17,975 --> 00:12:20,812
British and French troops
were occupying the

226
00:12:20,852 --> 00:12:23,428
city, and access was tightly restricted.

227
00:12:23,671 --> 00:12:27,543
A Red Cross doctor in Cairo
arranges a special train with the

228
00:12:27,583 --> 00:12:31,647
Egyptian Red Crescent to send part
of the medicine to Port Said.

229
00:12:31,832 --> 00:12:36,250
Another Red Cross delegate flies into Port
Said first on a French military plane,

230
00:12:36,290 --> 00:12:40,542
and gets officers of both countries to
agree to let the relief convoy through.

231
00:12:40,774 --> 00:12:44,280
The medicine arrived on November 16.

232
00:12:44,505 --> 00:12:48,353
For some, the Suez Crisis was a
ceasefire announced in Parliament ten

233
00:12:48,393 --> 00:12:52,298
days earlier — for others, it was
medicine that had only just arrived.

234
00:12:52,529 --> 00:12:56,851
The Red Cross also took on passing
news between families in the city.

235
00:12:58,736 --> 00:13:01,472
Britain's November books are now out too.

236
00:13:01,562 --> 00:13:05,332
Reserves fell by 279 million
dollars in a single month.

237
00:13:05,422 --> 00:13:08,926
They dropped below the two-billion-dollar
line Britain had wanted to hold.

238
00:13:09,256 --> 00:13:12,851
On December 10, Britain drew
about 561 million dollars

239
00:13:12,891 --> 00:13:16,149
from the International
Monetary Fund, and secured

240
00:13:16,189 --> 00:13:19,515
a separate credit line it
could draw on if needed.

241
00:13:19,801 --> 00:13:24,240
That is how the price of two dollars
eighty cents to the pound was defended.

242
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:28,541
But what a London driver really
wanted to know was something else.

243
00:13:28,784 --> 00:13:32,923
How much petrol could I put in this month?

244
00:13:33,294 --> 00:13:37,205
On December 17, motor fuel
rationing begins in Britain.

245
00:13:37,372 --> 00:13:41,057
The two hundred miles a month from our
opening scene belongs to this moment.

246
00:13:41,492 --> 00:13:46,130
Only on December 22 did British and
French forces complete their withdrawal.

247
00:13:46,319 --> 00:13:49,682
The UN-contracted salvage
fleet of 32 vessels finished

248
00:13:49,722 --> 00:13:52,833
clearing the canal in April
of the following year.

249
00:13:53,334 --> 00:13:56,649
Now let's look at the Brumaire
in September one more time.

250
00:13:56,877 --> 00:14:00,069
Open the article again, and the
white tanker's name is there,

251
00:14:00,109 --> 00:14:03,831
and so is the name of the official
who shook hands with those boarding.

252
00:14:04,064 --> 00:14:06,880
Yet the man who actually guided that

253
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:09,894
ship remains only "an Egyptian pilot."

254
00:14:10,230 --> 00:14:13,690
Of everyone in the Suez Crisis,
he is the one I wonder about most.

255
00:14:13,995 --> 00:14:17,158
The British prime minister
left a parliamentary record;

256
00:14:17,198 --> 00:14:19,836
the American president
left a call transcript.

257
00:14:20,176 --> 00:14:24,252
What shows us what that pilot did,
in the September 15 report,

258
00:14:24,292 --> 00:14:29,099
is one white ship heading north that
morning with thirty more behind her.

259
00:14:29,461 --> 00:14:33,760
Britain knew exactly how
important that waterway was.

260
00:14:33,884 --> 00:14:36,185
What it miscalculated was something else.

261
00:14:36,579 --> 00:14:40,975
Britain could decide to put
troops at the mouth of the canal.

262
00:14:41,117 --> 00:14:44,721
But to get one tanker safely
through tomorrow morning,

263
00:14:44,761 --> 00:14:48,096
in the end, you needed
someone who knew the water.

